Approach 2: Better (Double Traversal)
Intuition

Treat the array as if it were duplicated.

1 2 1

↓

1 2 1 1 2 1

For every index, search ahead until n elements.

Still quadratic but easier to understand.

Java
class Solution {

    public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;
        int[] ans = new int[n];

        Arrays.fill(ans, -1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            for (int j = i + 1; j < i + n; j++) {

                if (nums[j % n] > nums[i]) {

                    ans[i] = nums[j % n];
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Complexity
Time: O(n²)
Space: O(1)

Approach 3: Monotonic Stack (Most Optimal)
Intuition

Use a Monotonic Decreasing Stack.

Since the array is circular, iterate twice (2 × n).

Use

i % n

to wrap around.

Only fill answers during the second half (i < n).

Algorithm
